_model: other_work
---
title: How this site is built
---
order: 3
---
body:

This edition is a static site. Every page is generated ahead of time from plain
text files, so there is no database and nothing to query when you open a
proposition — the browser receives finished HTML, and the only code that runs is
the diagram library.

### Where the pages came from

Dr. Joyce wrote his edition between 1996 and 2020 as hand-authored HTML, one
file per proposition, definition, or essay, with a Java applet supplying the
figures. Those files are the source this edition was converted from: **551
legacy pages**, each read, split into its parts, and rewritten as structured
text.

The conversion was not a reformat. Joyce's HTML mixes the statement of a
proposition, its proof, and his commentary in one flow, distinguished only by
typography. Here they are separate fields, which is what allows a proof to be
walked one step at a time while the commentary stays put. His `<applet>` blocks
became [`geomlib`](/geomlib/) canvases, and his cross-references — written as
links to files like `propI47.html` — became citation tokens that resolve to
whatever the URL happens to be.

### What the pages are made of

The site is built with [Lektor](https://www.getlektor.com/), a static generator
that lets a project define its own content types. Thirteen are defined here, and
the counts show the shape of the work:

| Content type | Pages |
|---|---|
| Proposition | 465 |
| Definition | 131 |
| Definition bundle | 32 |
| Section index | 24 |
| Other works | 17 |
| Book introduction | 13 |
| Prematter essay | 6 |
| Postulate | 5 |
| Common notion | 5 |

A **proposition** is not one blob of text. It carries its statement, its proof,
its marginal justifications, its figures, and Joyce's guide commentary as
separate fields, and the page template assembles them. That separation is what
makes the interactive layer possible.

The **definition bundle** is a case where Joyce's own structure had to be
modelled rather than flattened. He often wrote several definitions in a single
file — `defV1.html` holds both V.Def.1 and V.Def.2 — with one shared commentary
covering the group. Each definition here gets its own page, while a hidden
bundle page holds the shared guide, so a citation to V.Def.2 lands somewhere
sensible without duplicating Joyce's commentary across pages.

Some pages to look at: a [proposition](/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI47/)
with a proof walk-through, a [definition](/elements/books/bookI/definitions/defI15_18/)
from a bundle, a [book introduction](/elements/books/bookI/), and one of
[Joyce's other works](/other-works/compass-geometry/).

### The three extensions written for it

Lektor was extended with three small plugins, each answering a problem this
particular text raised.

**Citations and element references.** Joyce's prose is dense with
cross-references, and hand-writing links for them would not survive a change of
URL scheme. Instead the source says `@I.47` and the plugin resolves it. The same
plugin builds the reverse index — the "this proposition is used in…" list at the
foot of a page is not written by hand anywhere; it is collected from every
citation pointing at that page. It also handles the `{AB}` references that light
up the figures, described on the [Using](/geomlib/using/) page.

**Mathematics.** The few places needing set mathematics are rendered at build
time rather than in the browser, so no math library is shipped to readers.

**Roman-numeral URLs.** The smallest plugin, and the most particular. Book X's
definitions restart their numbering in three subsections, so their addresses end
in fragments like `defX.III.6`. Python treats a trailing `.6` as a file
extension it does not recognise, and the development server offered the pages as
downloads instead of displaying them. Forty lines to teach it that these are
pages. It is the kind of thing that only appears when a text's own numbering
outlives the assumptions of the tools carrying it.

### Checking the figures

The diagrams are the part most likely to break quietly: a figure can be
misspecified and simply not appear, leaving a page that looks finished. So every
figure on the site is exercised before anything is published — each page's
diagram code is run against the real library, and a page that throws, or that
names an element which does not exist, stops the deploy. **634 diagrams** across
**524 pages** are checked this way.

That check earned its place immediately: it found a Book IV figure whose element
declarations had been transposed during conversion, so the page had been quietly
falling back to a static image since it was written.
